    "proof": "Put pi=sqrt(2), a=2*pi-1, epsilon=1+pi and delta=3+pi=epsilon*a. For a primitive r=(x,y), reflection integrality is q(r)|2I(r), where I(r)=(a*x,(y,Lambda22)). Away from pi, 3 and delta the spatial Gram matrix is unimodular, so a prime divisor of q would divide both x and y, contradicting primitivity. At delta the exponent is zero or one; when it is one, y lies in delta*Lambda22. At the inert prime 3, the Smith factor 6 permits exponent zero or one and the latter requires G*y divisible by 3. At pi, evenness and the Smith factors 2,6 give the complete exponents 0,2,3,4. For y=u+pi*v, exponent 3 requires G*u even and exponent 4 requires both G*u and G*v even. The local choices are independent. Since Z[sqrt(2)] has class number one and every totally-positive unit is a square, the sixteen products of the four dyadic representatives 1,2,4+2*pi,4 with optional factors 3 and delta are the complete canonical primitive mirror-norm alphabet. For a delta-class write y=delta*z and apply the pi/3 gates to z."
  },
